[Music]  italian skiers are back on the slopes  and a place that last year was the  epicenter of the pandemic in europe  as covet 19 ravaged northern italy the  country took tough measures to stave off  disaster  now as case numbers are spiking in  neighboring countries italy will place  more restrictions on unvaccinated people  we want  what lessons can italy teach us about  dealing with the coronavirus pandemic  welcome to your covet 19 special i'm  chris colbert in berlin  last year's dramatic pictures and  europe's second highest death toll  linked to covet 19 seemed to have had an  impact italy already has one of the  strictest anti-infection regiments in  the world and with cases rising again  the country is now barring unvaccinated  citizens from much of public life  time for a booster shot an invitation  message is sent to all those who qualify  for one italian care home staff teachers  and high-risk groups got theirs first  over 40s are up next  there's been an effective vaccination  campaign in italy which is not common  the fact so many people have been  vaccinated is good for everyone  it's also important that each of us pay  attention to where we go  that we always wear a mask and we keep  our distance  all these factors along with  vaccinations have contributed to  controlling the number of infections in  italy  on public transport only those  vaccinated those who've recovered from  infection or have been tested are  allowed on board  the same goes for workplaces  companies that don't check for a  so-called green pass face fines and  employees without it could have their  pay cut  people must pay for their own corona  virus tests in order to push more  italians to get vaccinated  in recent days the number of infections  has risen in italy but there are far  fewer seriously ill people compared to a  year ago  only 475 of 9 000 intensive care beds  are occupied  italy remains optimistic as winter  approaches  only those vaccinated recently recovered  or who've tested negative are allowed on  the country's ski slopes but italy is  hopeful that christmas can be more  relaxed than last year  let's take a closer look at what italy  has been through and which conclusions  we can draw from that together with  walter ricciardi he's a hygiene and  public health professor and with the  intensive care physician enrico storti  bonjourno senori welcome to both of you  enrico let me start with you how hopeful  are you that you and your colleagues  will have less to do over the holidays  i think that we can say that  now the situation is completely  different uh  compared with what we experimented last  year i mean we have of course the virus  circulating in our country  but thanks to the vaccination protocol  that italy  decide to to carry on  i think that now the impact on the  hospital and on the icu particularly by  my perspective is much more  easier to be controlled and so our daily  practically is  absolutely better compared with last  year  walter over to you more than two months  ago italy imposed some of the strictest  anti-covet measures in the world  practically all workers in the country  needed to show proof of vaccination  recovery or a negative test to get to  their job or lose pay now starting  monday the country's super green pass  will basically bar unvaccinated citizens  from much of public life why  because we learned the lesson that this  virus must be anticipated and not  followed and the delta variant really  changed the game so we understood that  only with very strict measures we could  anticipate the spread of the virus and  we could avoid the situation of having a  country that had to be locked again so  essentially this is uh really the  situation as it is now is much better  compared to other countries because we  advance these measures not follow them  how have people been reacting to this  new rule walter  after all very well i mean you know the  vast majority of the countries is  following the rule i would say  more than 90 percent has been vaccinated  and more than 90 percent is falling very  strictly the rule there is a tiny  minority which is very noisy unfortunate  that with some media generating a lot of  anxiety in the country because of this  tiny and noisy minority but after all  the countries is behaving  enrico back to you you said the  situation today  differs greatly from the one  last year  nevertheless we see infections rising  not only throughout europe but also in  italy how worried are you about new  variants occurring like omicron  uh i mean uh as professor richard uh  already said i think that uh we did our  best to be uh ready and to get ready  about this new uh spread of the  different  virus i mean different types of virus  and of course we are worried but i think  that now  we have to to better understand  exactly what are what are the  characteristics of this new variant and  basically uh starting from what we know  now i think that we can say that is a  sort of it's it's powerful in terms of  in  capacity to infect but we didn't know  exactly if this characteristics is able  to sustain a different impact on the on  the illness itself so i think that we  are ready we are now trying to do what  we can actively do  and we are still doing that so we are  not worried but of course we have to be  very careful about what is going on  walter back to you with more than 130  000 deaths linked to covet 19 italy has  the second highest  death toll in europe we've seen the  pictures of coffins being stacked onto  army trucks how much have these images  had an impact on the public and their  behavior during the pandemic  they had an impact absolutely they  really shocked the country and then made  all the people aware that this was not  an ordinary flu this was a serious  disease to be taken into account  particularly because the first wave  struck the best part of the country the  most developed the richest the most  equipped so we were shocked and people  understood that this was serious  unfortunately the second and the third  wave we underestimated the importance of  keeping these measures alive but now we  have learned the lesson  walter what needs to be key  for italy when moving forward in this  pandemic  we believe as dr sorti said that  vaccinating is still the most important  pillar of action but not only we have to  keep a safe distance we have to keep our  universal masking we have to keep  hygiene we have to be very careful in in  mass gatherings so the combination of  the measures is the only way to manage  this pandemic and we are going to do  that  enrico back to you  even with all the precautions some  people will end up being hospitalized  now at the beginning of the pandemic  treatment was scarce how well are you  equipped today  uh no let me say that i think that we  can say that we are we now or better  know this uh this infection this uh the  the direct consequences that this  infection is able to bring  and uh so i feel myself and i can i can  tell you that all the icu community uh  physicians in  in italy we feel ourselves confident  that we can we have  we have all the tools that we need  to control this business unfortunately i  think that in the near future will be  more would be important trying to get  some drugs  and not only how to ventilate and how to  preserve the  patient's lungs  to have a better outcome of this  infection let me say that if we compare  now our treatment  compared with what we did  and what we can do at that time one year  ago i think that now we uh  we have a much more concrete standard  that uh i think it's uh really enough to  treat these patients  intensive care physician enrico storti  and public health professor walter  ricciardi  thank you to your both mille grazie  senori  you're welcome  and now it's time for another one of  your questions here's our science  correspondent derek williams  can you tell us more about the effects  of long covid in kids  one of the few upsides if you will of  sars kobe 2 is that at least during the  active infection  um children are largely spared serious  outcomes like  hospitalization  and death  but that doesn't mean that some of them  won't eventually pay a price um  estimates for  what percentage of children suffer  symptoms associated with long covid in  the weeks and the months following an  infection  they're kind of all over the place  but most researchers in the field seem  to agree now that it's significant  although  recent data indicates thankfully it  might not be as common as we once  thought  some early research postulated  that postcoded symptoms might affect up  to half of all the kids who got it  that estimate has dropped considerably  with a larger recent study  roughly finding more like one in seven  kids are still reporting symptoms  over three months after a covet  infection just like in adults those long  covid symptoms have also been very  wide-ranging in kids um the most serious  reported effect is called  multi-system inflammatory syndrome in  children or  misc  which can lead to organ failure it's  fortunately pretty rare  there have only been around five and a  half thousand cases of it reported in  the u.s so far for instance  more common effects are sometimes but  not always similar  to those reported by adults like  fatigue or labored breathing or  headaches  or changes to the sense of smell and  taste  neurological symptoms like brain fog or  or difficulties concentrating are also  widely reported  some studies show that vaccines appear  to help cut the risk in an adult  of developing long covid if they  experience a breakthrough infection  maybe by as much as half  but  there isn't enough data yet on whether  they also  reduce the risk of it in younger kids  we just haven't been vaccinating them  long enough to know  [Music]  and staying with vaccines the oxford  astrazeneca jab has been credited with  saving millions of lives but some  countries suspended its use after an  extremely rare side effect was reported  that may cause blood clots in some  recipients now researchers in britain  and the united states may have found  what causes the clotting a protein in  the blood is attracted to a key  component of the vaccine  causing a chain reaction with the immune  system astrazeneca scientists took part  in the research and say they are looking  at ways to eliminate the side effect  that's our show for now for more  information check out our website at  dw.com covet19 i'm chris coburn berlin  thanks for watching stay safe  you

